<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajanta Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–5th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ajanta Caves are about thirty Buddhist rock-cut monasteries and prayer halls in a horseshoe-shaped gorge of the Waghora river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The earliest were cut around the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE; most of the rest date from the late 5th century, under the Vakataka king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a> and his nobles. Their wall paintings of the Buddha's lives are the finest surviving ancient Indian paintings, and the site is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves.jpg" alt="Ajanta Caves" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Soman assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_(63).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
